{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"04c115799c57e4de","type":"text","text":"API","x":-272,"y":-13,"width":461,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"b761dcb224631668","type":"text","text":"- An API (Application Programming Interface) is a software interface that allows two applications to communicate with each other\n- APIs are essential not just for network automation, but for all kinds of applications.\n- In SDN architecture, APIs are used to communicate between apps and the SDN controller (via the NBI), and between the SDN contoller and the network devices (via the SBI).\n- The northbound interface (NBI) typically uses REST APIs\n- NETCONF and RESTCONF are popular southbound APIs","x":-272,"y":47,"width":461,"height":393},
		{"id":"a63b988c5e9e6cb6","type":"text","text":"HTTP Verbs","x":-880,"y":1280,"width":500,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"2fe1499648a6b64d","type":"text","text":"- HTTP uses verbs (aka methods) that map to these CRUD operations.\n- REST APIs typically use HTTPS","x":-880,"y":1340,"width":500,"height":120},
		{"id":"2a577e87909eb327","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260812144532.png","x":-880,"y":1460,"width":498,"height":280},
		{"id":"0be5e91a23a1641a","type":"text","text":"CRUD Operations","x":-880,"y":730,"width":500,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"668327d8fea4412a","type":"text","text":"- CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) refers to the operations we perform using REST APIs\n- CREATE operations are used to create new variables and set their initial values.\n\t- create variable \"ip address\" and set the value to \"10.1.1.1\".\n- READ - used to retrieve the value of a variable.\n\t- what is the value of variable \"ip_address\"\n- UPDATE - used to change the value of a variable.\n\t- change the value of variable \"ip_address\" to 10.2.3.4.\n- DELETE - operations are used to delete variables.\n\t- delete variable \"ip_address\"","x":-880,"y":790,"width":500,"height":380},
		{"id":"9465737abf66126e","type":"text","text":"HTTP Request","x":-880,"y":1840,"width":500,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"d63976058b69d1b9","type":"text","text":"- When an HTTP client sends a request to an HTTP server, the HTTP header includes information like this:\n\t- An HTTP verb (ie. GET)\n\t- A URI (Uniform Resource Identified), indicating the resource it is trying to access.\n- HTTP request can include additional headers which pass additional information to the server.\n\t- Check the list at https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers\n- An example would be an Accept header, which informs the server about the type(s) of data that can be sent back to the client\n\t- Accept: application/json or Accept: applicatoins/xml\n- You can also view standard HTTP header fields with some examples at\n\t- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_HTTP_header_fields\n- When a REST client makes an API call (request) to a REST server, it will send an HTTP request like the one above\n\t- REST APIs don't have to use HTTP for communication, although HTTP is the most common choice","x":-882,"y":1900,"width":500,"height":600},
		{"id":"394a647415b15da9","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260812144759.png","x":-882,"y":2500,"width":500,"height":184},
		{"id":"1b881039d3d66a9e","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260812152428.png","x":-882,"y":2684,"width":500,"height":33},
		{"id":"7b1fbb90b8016706","type":"text","text":"HTTP Request","x":-898,"y":2820,"width":500,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"bf97c22694fafc78","type":"text","text":"- The server's response will include a status code indicating if the request succeeded or failed, as well as other details.\n- The first digit indicates the class of the response:\n\t- 1xx informational\n\t\t- request was received, continuing process\n\t\t- 102 = Processing - server has received req and processing it but resp not yet available\n\t- 2xx successful\n\t\t- request successfully received, understood and accepted\n\t\t- 200 = OK - req succeeded\n\t\t- 201 = Created = req succeeded and new resource was created (resp for POST)\n\t- 3xx redirection\n\t\t- further action needs to be taken in order to complete the request\n\t\t- 301 = Moved permanently - req resource has been moved, and the server indicates its new location \n\t- 4xx client error\n\t\t- the request contains bad syntax or cannot be fulfilled\n\t\t- 403 = Unauthorized - client must authenticate to get a response\n\t\t- 404 = Not Found - requested resource not found\n\t- 5xx server error\n\t\t- the server failed to fulfill an apparently valid request\n\t\t- 500 = Internal Server Error - server encountered something unexpected that it doesn't know how to handle\n- resp codes: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Status\n- expected resp codes to each resp\n\t- https://www.restapitutorial.com/lessons/httpmethods.html","x":-900,"y":2880,"width":500,"height":980},
		{"id":"a727c0889489f08a","type":"text","text":"REST: Stateless","x":679,"y":1370,"width":500,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"e5c4a98b22c66cd3","type":"text","text":"- REST APIs are stateless\n- Each API exchange is a separate event, independent of all past exchanges between the client and server\n\t- server does not store information about previous requests from the client to determine how it should respond to new requests.\n- If authentication is required, this means that the client must authenticate with the server for each request it makes.\n- ex: TCP is an example of a stateful protocol\n- ex: UDP is an example of a stateless protocol\n- Although REST APIs use HTTP, which uses TCP (stateful) as its Layer 4 protocol, HTTP and REST APIs themselves aren't stateful. The functions of each layer are separate","x":679,"y":1430,"width":500,"height":420},
		{"id":"ef045230aed86b65","type":"text","text":"REST: Cacheable or Non-Cacheable","x":1274,"y":1370,"width":500,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"e1767499b0e4ce06","type":"text","text":"- REST APIs must support caching of data\n- Caching refers to storing data for future use\n\t- for example, your computer might cache many elements of a web page so that it doesn't have to retrieve the entire page every time you visit it.\n\t- This improves performance for the client and reduces the load on the server\n- Not all resources have to be cacheable, but cacheable resources MUST be declared as cacheable.","x":1274,"y":1430,"width":500,"height":420},
		{"id":"3d93e2f95d5c09a3","type":"text","text":"- REST APIs use a client-server architecture.\n- The client uses API calls (HTTP requests) to access the resources on the server\n- The separation between the client and server means they can both change and evolve independently of each other\n\t- When the client application changes or the server applications changes, the interface between them must not break.","x":94,"y":1430,"width":500,"height":420},
		{"id":"c8691743a1764230","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260812161623.png","x":94,"y":1850,"width":500,"height":139},
		{"id":"579eebec11c87556","type":"text","text":"REST API (Representational State Transfer)","x":679,"y":730,"width":500,"height":68,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"23239fba5dcfe541","type":"text","text":"- REST APIs AKA as REST-based APIs or RESTful APIs\n\t- isn't a specific API. Instead, it describes a set of rules about how the API should work.\n- Six constraints of RESTful architecture are:\n\t- Uniform Interface\n\t- Client-Server\n\t- Stateless\n\t- Cacheable or non-cacheable\n\t- Layered System\n\t- Code-on-demand (optional)\n- details:\n\t- https://restfulapi.net/rest-architectural-constraints/\n- For apps to communicate over a network, networking protocols must be used to facilitate those communications\n\t- For REST APIs, HTTP(S) is the most common choice","x":679,"y":798,"width":500,"height":472},
		{"id":"d88a9d9f6aff526f","type":"text","text":"REST: Client-Server","x":94,"y":1370,"width":500,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"02a4fcb72272adbe","type":"text","text":"REST APIs Calls using Cisco DevNet","x":679,"y":2200,"width":500,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"92fb722f398f558d","type":"text","text":"- Cisco DevNet is Cisco's developer program to help developers and IT professionals who want to write applications and develop integrations with Cisco products, platforms, and APIs\n- DevNet offers lots of free resources such as courses, tutorials, labs, sandboxes, documentation, etc. to learn about automation and develop your skills.\n- DevNet cert for automation after CCNA\n- Cisco DNA center Sandbox to send a REST API call using POSTMAN.\n\t- DNA center is one of Cisco's SDN controllers. \n\t\t- make acct on developer.cisco.com\n\t- Postman is a platform for building and using APIs\n\t\t- make acct on postman and download the desktop app\n- DevNet tutorial\n\t- https://developer.cisco.com/docs/dna-center/#!getting-started","x":679,"y":2260,"width":500,"height":480}
	],
